A||N||15||87ECO5||Need help bringing '76 Corvette back to life||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||06-05-2001||03:21 PM||||I've spoken to a few of my buddies here and they've all given me some good advice.  Thought I'd see what others had to say.<P>My wife’s grandfather has a ’76 Corvette that’s been sitting in his garage for two years now without ever being driven – AND IT WAS NEVER “WINTERIZED”.<P>He called me last Sunday morning to ask if I’d “bring it back to life” for him.  He offered to flatbed it up to my garage (from North Carolina) and he is going to pay for anything it needs.  Of course, after I choked on my breakfast, I offered to take on the project.<P>Now, I need some idea what I’ll need to do to “bring it back to life.”  I’ve had several folks tell me the same thing:  drain and replace all fluids – including the gas, and inspect (and replace as needed) all rubber seals and hoses.”<P>Can anyone suggest other things that should be done?  I want to do this right (regardless of the rumor floating around that I may end up with the car).<P>I haven’t seen the car in eight years but I’m told it’s in pretty sad shape now – appearance-wise.  I guess the front and rear clips are literally cracking and falling off of the car.  What I know about the car is:<P>- ~40,000 original miles<BR>- base engine (L-48) with auto tranny<P>Any help is greatly appreciated.  Thank you in advance.||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||06-05-2001||03:46 PM||||He'll probably try to start it to get it up on the truck, so have him check all this stuff first. Especially the oil and trans fluid.<P>Oughta be a piece of cake to get it running good...no computer or sensor issues to deal with.||||reg||1
Z||000002||buds||06-05-2001||04:30 PM||||I bought a '74 vette 1.5 years ago, it also was sitting(6 years)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> I thought-ya just a few little things and I'm driven a vette! wrong! Had to rebuild motor, completely replace braking system(vettes are bad for the piston bores rusting in the caliper,$150 ea canadian)new interior,all new front suspension and steering components,heater core,rims and tires and on and on. I'm now drivin' it almost everyday and lovin it. <A HREF="http://www.corvetteforum.con" TARGET=_blank>www.corvetteforum.com</A>  helped out alot(also buds on there)let me know if you need any help along the way!<P>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1270757&a=9406956&p=49359931">  <p>[This message has been edited by buds (edited 06-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierospeeder||06-05-2001||06:42 PM||||I would remove all the plugs and put some marvel oil about a tablespoon or 2.  put the plugs back in and leave it in overnight.  Let it eat away the rust if any on the rings and pistons.  <P>get a siphon those cheap ones at the car parts store and siphon the gas out.  Put new gas in.  Change the oil and trans fluid.  the differential and fill it up till it pours out.  <P>you might need a new battery.  check the fluids, draining the radiator would be a good thing, new hoses and belts after you get it running.  <P>Get a wrench and turn the engine over at the crankshaft pulley and see if it turns over easily.  Ive heard if the engines locked up, you can break a ring.  <P>What i do is this with my cars, disconnect the plug going to the coil and crank the engine until the oil light turns off.  I put the plug back to the coil and start the car up.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||06-05-2001||06:46 PM||||Scott--Sounds like you've really got a job ahead of you.  The email made it sound easier than everyone's telling you.  At least you won't have to remortgage the house for parts money.  The bad part about this is the fact that you absolutely, positively, without a doubt can't stand Corvettes.  Since this will pose a problem, feel free to leave your (sorry...your in laws) Vette at my house.<P>MM<p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 06-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Philphine||06-05-2001||07:43 PM||||i put my '74 in storage for what i thought would be a few months but ended up being about a year and a half, so i didn't do any of the things you should for long term storage. i was worse about cars then (not like i know a lot now). i just took out the plugs and sprayed i think slick 50 spray lube in the cylinders and started it up. guess you wouldn't want to be that cavalier, but the engines seem to be able to take a lot. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||87ECO5||07-24-2001||03:41 PM||||Well, the Vette arrived (not necessarily in one piece) last Thursday afternoon.  Here's my initial assessment:<P>Minus the front and rear clips, the car is in really good shape.  The interior has one small tear on the driver's seat but is in otherwise perfect condition.  The body is clean but has some faded paint.  Mechanically, the car appears to be sound (although I'm sure I'll find some things once I start digging).<P>What I know so far:<P>The front and rear urethane bumpers have dried out and cracked, and half of them are missing!  I'm not kidding!  Most of the damage occurred during the drive up from North Carolina.  The buffeting from being at highway speeds caused pieces of the clips to fly off (this is what I meant by "not necessarily in one piece").  By the time the car reached my driveway, half of the front and ALL of the rear were gone!  In fact, as it was being backed into the driveway, the license plate fell off (glad that didn't happen somewhere along the way).  Gus (the owner, and Lisa's grandfather) wants GM replacement urethane clips installed.  I checked a recent Ecklers catalog.  Found a set for ... are you sitting down? ... <B>$1100 PLUS shipping!</B>  Wow!  Glad he's paying for everything!<P>I've found some oil under the left-rear tire.  I believe the shocks are leaking.<P>The antenna mast is badly bent.  Found a replacement in Ecklers for $20.<P>I'm planning to do the basic tune-up thing (plugs, wires, cap, rotor, filters, etc.) plus change all fluids - including the fuel in the tank, inspect/replace any bad hoses or seals, etc.<P>That's about it.  I've got it for two months.<P>More to come ... stay tuned.<p>[This message has been edited by 87ECO5 (edited 07-24-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000007||rogergarrison||07-24-2001||07:59 PM||||I usually use the fiberglass replacements when I do Corvettes from mid 70's, they take a little triming and glass work to fit rite, but they dont sag like the OEMs. I think I paid $235 for the front for a 74 a couple months ago. I get most stuff from Eklers too. The guy that owns it hates selling to small garages, I dont know why. Just seems like he likes big mass production resto shops. I cant remember what I paid for last glass rear. The front fenders if you have to replace them are a HUGE pain to get to fit even close. hoods wont follow curve, around the front of door opening and winshield really sucks too. I never seen a factory one that really fit right. If your trying to make it look original, I found a neat trick to make the fender and quarter panel look OEM with the barely visible seam. I did one that judges checked over for Bloomington Gold. Fooled them, they certified it was almost completely original paint   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 07-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Santa Cruzer||07-24-2001||11:30 PM||||aren't the 74-76 vettes the cheapest one's you can buy? from what i hear there very cheap to get,and even if you pur money into them there not worth much..<p>[This message has been edited by Santa Cruzer (edited 07-24-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000009||87ECO5||07-25-2001||07:18 AM||||Most folks will say the mid-'70's cars were the low point of Vette history.  Lower compression engines with emissions systems added resulted in the lowest horsepower rating since the '53 'Blue Flame' inline 6 (the "high-performance" L-82 engine was limited to around 210-230 horsepower and the "base" L-48 engine was a measly 150!).<P>However, I believe the C3 (1968-1982) was a beautiful design and the 1980 L-82 is third on my list of favorite Vettes.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Tigger||07-25-2001||08:52 AM||||Hmmm...   A Corvette owner asking for help from a bunch of Fiero enthusiasts...<P>Waddya say, should we really help him?<P>After all, it has been said that the Corvette was one of the reasons they stopped making the Fiero.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">||||reg||13
Z||000011||Tigger||07-25-2001||08:54 AM||||hehe, I'm just kidding 87ECO5.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>The Vettes from those years were underpowered but that's something that can be fixed.<P>Sounds like a nice project vette, good luck!  <p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 07-25-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000012||88CoupeV6||07-25-2001||11:42 AM||||I've been bringing my dads 82 back to life. Car only has 40,000 on it and it looks bad from just sitting I keep telling him that sportscars are meant to be driven. The garage had moisture problems and the car was moldy inside <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> The right t-top was so corroded at the rear pin It took 2 hours to remove  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  It has been a good 200 miles this year, It needs more driving time. I took it for its sticker. What a hard car to feel in control of. too small a cockpit Sitting on the drivers door with the seat laying way back and a hood as long as a fiero. I get a lot of looks when cruising by though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">   <BR><B>Also 88 Yellow GT</B> :D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||7
